NAME
    chd - convert disc games to CHD, verify them, and list their status

SYNOPSIS
    chd ls [<path>...]
    chd convert <path>... [--fast] [-j <threads>] [--output <folder> | --beside]
    chd check [<path>...]
    chd wait [chd<n>]

DESCRIPTION
    Manages CHD disc compression for the emusen.library module. Each
    <path> names a file or folder relative to the library root
    (AppSettings.RomDirectory), using '/' separators (for example, psx,
    "psx/Rayman (USA)", SCD, or / for the whole library). Omitting <path>
    on 'ls' and 'check' covers the whole library.

    Supported disc systems are PlayStation, PlayStation 2, and Sega CD
    (cue sheets with tracks, lone raw tracks, ISO images, or zips holding
    one). Console routing follows the library scanner and falls back to
    the nearest console folder above the file (psx, ps2, segacd).

    The library is read-only unless '--beside' is passed to 'convert'.
    Outputs overlapping the library are refused before anything is written.
    Even with '--beside', originals are never modified, renamed, or
    removed.

    'convert' invokes the pinned MAME chdman binary shipped beside the
    platform library (verified by SHA-256 against
    Platform/library-tool/chdman.pin; a mismatch aborts before writing).
    Each disc is written to a partial file and atomically renamed into
    place only after three checks pass in the same run:

        1  chdman verify (all hunks decompressed and overall SHA-1 checked).
        2  Every track or sector rebuilt through EmuSen's own disc reader
           and checked by CRC-32, MD5, and SHA-1 against the original.
        3  Disc geometry equality: TOC, track starts/pauses, lead-out, and
           disc type for CDs; sector count and dual-layer break for DVDs.

    Each converted disc receives a linked package under
    /Packages/<console>/ recording the CHD path, original dump hashes,
    codecs, and verification SHA-1s. Already converted discs whose CHD and
    originals match their package records are skipped. Each disc requires
    free space equal to the original size plus 64 MiB. One failed disc
    never stops a batch.

    By default CHDs are written under /Compressed (or
    AppSettings.ChdOutputDirectory when configured). CHDs outside
    /Compressed are referenced by absolute path in their package manifest.

OPTIONS
    ls [<path>...]
        List disc games and status (converted, not converted, changed, or
        missing) along with original and compressed byte sizes from
        package records.

    convert <path>...
        Convert disc games under <path> to CHD and create linked packages.

    --fast
        Use Zstandard codecs (cdzs,cdfl for CDs; zstd for DVDs) instead of
        the defaults (cdlz,cdzl,cdfl for CDs; lzma,zlib,huff,flac for
        DVDs) for faster decode at slightly larger file size.

    -j <threads>
        Set chdman compression thread count (must be > 0; defaults to all
        available CPU threads).

    --output <folder>
        Write CHDs into sandbox path <folder> for this command only.
        Cannot overlap the library or be combined with --beside.

    --beside
        Write <stem>.chd next to each original inside the library using
        non-replacing rename. Skips discs where <stem>.chd already exists.
        Cannot be combined with --output.

    check [<path>...]
        Re-run all three verification checks against existing CHDs and
        their package records without reading the original dumps.

    wait [chd<n>]
        Follow a background chd job until completion.

DVDS
    A cue sheet or lone 2,352-byte-sector raw track is treated as a CD
    (chdman createcd) on all consoles. On PlayStation 2, a lone 2,048-byte
    .iso image is treated as a DVD (chdman createdvd), matching Sapphire's
    drive behaviour; PlayStation and Sega CD .iso images remain CDs. DVD
    packages record medium "dvd", sector count, and dual-layer break.

JOBS AND LOGS
    Long-running operations continue in the background as chd<n>. Use
    ps(1) to check progress, 'chd wait' to reattach, and kill(1) to stop
    (terminating chdman and deleting any partial file). Per-disc outcomes
    are appended to /Logs/chd_<day>.log.

EXAMPLES
    chd ls psx
    chd convert psx SCD
    chd convert ps2
    chd convert "psx/Crash Bandicoot (USA).bin" --fast
    chd convert psx --output /Compressed-PS1
    chd convert SCD --beside
    chd check psx
    ps
    chd wait chd1
    kill chd1

EXIT STATUS
    0   Command succeeded (or detached cleanly to a background job).
    1   Invalid arguments, path error, chdman hash mismatch, or job
        failure.

SEE ALSO
    pkg(1), rom(1), ps(1), kill(1), hier(7)
